For attempt >= 63 on 64-bit platforms, 1 << attempt overflows: it becomes negative at 63 and 0 from 64 on. Those values pass the d > 30 check and are returned as a zero or negative delay. A negative attempt is a run-time panic in Go, because the shift count is negative.

Suggestion: if attempt < 0 {
	attempt = 0
}
if attempt >= 5 {
	return 30
}
d := 1 << attempt
if d > 30 {
	d = 30
}
return d

`1 << attempt` overflows for large attempts. At 63 on 64-bit it becomes negative, and at 64 or more it becomes 0, so the cap check is skipped and the delay is wrong. A negative `attempt` also causes a runtime panic because Go rejects negative shift counts. Clamp `attempt` before shifting so the result always stays within 1..30.

Improved code:
if attempt < 0 {
		attempt = 0
	}
	if attempt >= 5 {
		return 30
	}
	d := 1 << attempt
	if d > 30 {
		d = 30
	}
	return d

