The fast path returns whatever the chunk's `then` returns, so a non-native thenable can make `next()` return `undefined` or a non-promise. If `then` throws synchronously, `next()` throws instead of rejecting and `inFlight` stays `true`, which hangs all queued requests forever. A misbehaving thenable can also invoke both callbacks, running the handlers more than once. Adopt the thenable into a native promise so `next()` always returns a promise and each handler runs at most once.

Improved code:
if (typeof then === 'function') {
  inFlight = true;
  return PromisePrototypeThen(
    new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      FunctionPrototypeCall(then, chunk, resolve, reject);
    }),
    onChunkFulfilled, onChunkRejected);
}

In `pump()`, a synchronous throw from a custom `then` escapes into the promise continuation. The request is then never settled and `inFlight` stays `true`, so the iterator deadlocks. A thenable that calls both callbacks, or calls one twice, would also finalize the iterator or drain the queue more than once. Wrap the call in a native promise so throws become rejections and settlement happens exactly once.

Improved code:
if (typeof then === 'function') {
  PromisePrototypeThen(
    new Promise((res, rej) => {
      FunctionPrototypeCall(then, chunk, res, rej);
    }),
    (value) => {
      inFlight = false;
      resolve({ done: false, value });
      if (queue !== null) drain();
    },
    (err) => {
      inFlight = false;
      settleError(err, reject);
      if (queue !== null) drain();
    });
  return;
}

