The first clause uses optional chaining (`previous?.isCJ`, `next?.isCJ`), which means `previous` or `next` can be undefined. The Korean-exclusion clause then reads `previous.kind` and `next.kind` without any guard. Suppose a whitespace node sits at a sentence boundary: `next` is a CJ word and `previous` is undefined (or the reverse). The first clause is true, so `previous.kind` is evaluated and throws a TypeError, crashing the formatter. Before this change that path was never reached, because `isLineBreakAmbiguous` did not exist and the newline check only ran when `proseWrap === "preserve"`.

Suggestion: function isLineBreakAmbiguous(path) {
  const { previous, next } = path;
