The old lexer stripped tag content, so `{%verbatim%}...{%endverbatim%}` worked. `_raw_start_re` now requires `\s+` after `{%`, so a template using `{%verbatim%}` or `{%comment%}` never reaches the raw-aware path. It falls through to plain `tag_re.split`, and since `create_token` no longer tracks verbatim state, `{{ x }}` inside the block is lexed as a VAR and rendered instead of being preserved. The end regexes `_comment_end_re` and `_verbatim_end_re` also require at least one non-newline whitespace character after `{%`. If a block was opened normally but closed with `{%endverbatim%}` or `{%endcomment%}`, the closing tag is never matched. The rest of the template is then swallowed as text, producing 'unclosed tag' errors or wrong output.

Suggestion: Use `\s*` where whitespace is optional: `{%\s*(?:comment|verbatim) ` for the start. Use `{%[^\S
]*endcomment[^\S
]*%}` and `{%[^\S
]*endverbatim[^\S
]*%}` for the ends. This matches the stripped-content semantics of the old lexer.

For a bare `verbatim` the closing regex needs `[^\S
]+` after `{%`. For a named verbatim the dynamically built regex uses `[^\S
]*`. So `{%endverbatim x%}` closes a named block but `{%endverbatim%}` does not close a bare one. The old lexer compared stripped content, so both forms closed. The named path also compiles a new regex on every named verbatim opening tag. `re` caches only a limited number of patterns, and templates with many named blocks pay the compile cost repeatedly.

Suggestion: Make both paths use the same optional-whitespace pattern. Build the closing regex through one helper, using `[^\S
]*` consistently.

