`CollapseLeadingSlashes` runs before the block that prepends '/' when `pattern[0] != '/'`. A target that begins with a backslash (for example `url="{R:1}"` with request `legacy/\evil.com`) passes through the normalizer unchanged because it does not start with '/'. It then becomes `/\evil.com`, which browsers resolve as the scheme-relative `//evil.com`. This leaves the open redirect the change is meant to close.

Suggestion: Move the call after the prepend block:
if (!pattern.Contains(Uri.SchemeDelimiter, StringComparison.Ordinal) && pattern[0] != '/') { pattern = '/' + pattern; }
pattern = UrlNormalizer.CollapseLeadingSlashes(pattern);

The check runs before `RedirectAction` and `RedirectRule` add a `/` to relative targets, but it only handles values that already start with `/`. A target such as `\example.com` or `\/example.com` (for example, `$1` capturing a backslash-prefixed path) is skipped here and then becomes `/\example.com` once the `/` is added. Browsers treat that as a scheme-relative URL, so the open redirect is still possible. Collapse any leading run of `/` or `\`, including one that starts with a backslash.

Improved code:
public static string CollapseLeadingSlashes(string url)
{
    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(url))
    {
        return url;
    }

    if (url[0] != '/' && url[0] != '\\')
    {
        return url;
    }

    if (url[0] == '/' && (url.Length < 2 || (url[1] != '/' && url[1] != '\\')))
    {
        return url;
    }

    var i = 0;
    while (i < url.Length && (url[i] == '/' || url[i] == '\\'))
    {
        i++;
    }

    return i == url.Length ? "/" : string.Concat("/", url.AsSpan(i));
}

